<section id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 data-animate="reveal">The questions everyone asks.</h2>
    <p class="subhead" data-animate="fade-up">If yours isn't here, ask via the waitlist signup — we'll add it.</p>

    <details class="faq" data-animate="fade-up">
      <summary>How is this different from Airfoil?</summary>
      <p>Airfoil ($35) is a 20-year-old Mac app from Rogue Amoeba. It does multi-protocol Mac→speakers streaming, and it's good at what it does. SuperAudio costs $19.99 (early-bird $12) — well under Airfoil's price — includes measurement-assisted cross-protocol sync that doesn't exist in any consumer product, and explicitly supports your stranded old hardware (gen-1 Sonos, B&amp;W A5/A7, AirPort Express, old Apple TVs). For the living room, our forthcoming Hub Pro plugs into your TV's HDMI ARC and fans <em>all</em> TV audio out to every speaker in sync — something no Mac app (or Apple TV app) can do, because TV apps can't capture protected streaming audio.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq" data-animate="fade-up">
      <summary>Does my music actually stay in sync between rooms?</summary>
      <p>Yes — with a one-time tune. AirPlay 1 receivers sync sample-accurately via our shared timing anchor; Sonos has a hidden internal buffer that we measure with your Mac's microphone. Your Mac plays a brief chirp from each speaker, measures how long the sound takes to reach where you're sitting, and gets the speakers close. You then set a static offset and nudge it by ear to lock it in at your seat — typically a single per-session adjustment, since the mic's spot isn't your spot. Sonos's buffer can drift between sessions, so you may re-tune occasionally. Fully hands-free continuous correction is in research — not a shipping promise. Planned iPhone-mic Room Tuning (M11) extends measurement with a walk-around for whole-room frequency correction.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq" data-animate="fade-up">
      <summary>What about my old Sonos? They've been dropping support for older devices.</summary>
      <p>SuperAudio drives any Sonos that still speaks UPnP — which is every model ever shipped, including the ones Sonos has demoted off their main app. Gen-1 Playbar, Connect, Connect:Amp, Beam, Play:5 — all supported. We don't depend on Sonos's app or cloud; if your Sonos is on the network, we drive it.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq" data-animate="fade-up">
      <summary>Does it work without an internet connection?</summary>
      <p>Yes. SuperAudio is 100% LAN-based. No cloud, no telemetry, no account required. Your music never leaves your house. Required: a Mac (or our Hub Stick) and a regular WiFi router. No cloud subscription, no Apple ID dance.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq" data-animate="fade-up">
      <summary>What about privacy?</summary>
      <p>No telemetry. No analytics. No "phoning home." Discovery is LAN-only via Bonjour. Audio streams stay local. We sell the software once and you own it.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq" data-animate="fade-up">
      <summary>I don't have a Mac. Can I still use it?</summary>
      <p>Yes — the $59 SuperAudio Hub Stick is a tiny preconfigured device that plugs into your router. Setup is one QR code on your phone. Then your iPhone, iPad, or smart TV can AirPlay to the Hub, which fans out to every speaker in your house. No Mac required.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq" data-animate="fade-up">
      <summary>When can I get it?</summary>
      <p>The Mac app is in active development, with a public beta opening in 2026. The Hub Stick is 3–4 months out, and the Hub Pro (TV → every speaker, via HDMI ARC) follows after that. <a href="#waitlist" data-cta="faq-waitlist">Get on the waitlist</a> and you'll hear the moment beta opens.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="waitlist">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="final-cta" data-animate="fade-up">
      <h2 data-animate="reveal">Be there when it ships.</h2>
      <p>Drop your email. We'll send one note when the Mac app's beta opens, and one more at the full release. Then we stop. <strong>No marketing emails, no newsletter, no nonsense.</strong></p>
      <form class="form" id="waitlist-form">
        <input type="email" name="email" placeholder="you@yourplace.com" required>
        <button type="submit" data-cta="waitlist-submit">Notify me</button>
      </form>
      <div class="note" id="waitlist-note">Two emails total. Ever.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var f = document.getElementById("waitlist-form");
    var note = document.getElementById("waitlist-note");
    if (!f) return;
    f.addEventListener("submit", function (e) {
      e.preventDefault();
      var input = f.querySelector('input[name="email"]');
      var btn = f.querySelector("button");
      var email = (input.value || "").trim();
      if (!email) return;
      btn.disabled = true; btn.textContent = "Joining…";
      var p = (window.SLF && window.SLF.joinWaitlist) ? window.SLF.joinWaitlist(email, "waitlist-hero") : Promise.reject();
      p.then(function (r) {
        if (r && (r.ok || r.status === 201)) { f.reset(); note.textContent = "You're on the list — two emails total, ever. ✓"; }
        else if (r && r.status === 409) { f.reset(); note.textContent = "You're already on the list. ✓"; }
        else { note.textContent = "Hmm, that didn't go through — try again in a moment."; }
      }).catch(function () {
        note.textContent = "Hmm, that didn't go through — try again in a moment.";
      }).finally(function () { btn.disabled = false; btn.textContent = "Notify me"; });
    });
  })();
</script>
</Base>
